{
  "text": " Imagine you leave Earth and travel through space at almost the speed of light and come back. You look at your clock and only five years have passed, but when you look at Earth, you realize 10 years have passed. So what's the problem? Because how can two clocks measure different amounts of time? And which time is actually right? Here's where Einstein's physics gets weird. You see, both clocks are right. Time doesn't pass at exactly the same rate for everyone. The faster you move relative to someone else, the less time can pass on your clock compared with theirs. So from your point of view, your five years feel completely normal. But when you return, people on Earth have aged much more than you have. And this is where it sounds impossible. If motion is relative, shouldn't Earth look like it's moving away from you too? So shouldn't Earth's clock run slower? Yes. And that's exactly where the real answer comes in. You can't just compare the two clocks while they're moving apart. In this case, the traveler has to turn around, come back, and then compare the clocks at the same place again. And once they do, the clocks can show different elapsed times. So nothing really broke, or no clock malfunctioned. The difference rather comes from the paths they took through space time. Two people can start together, take different journeys, and meet again, having experienced different amounts of time.",
